---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import loopRight from '../assets/img/practice-9.jpg';
import loopCompare from '../assets/img/practice-11.jpg';

const game = '/practice/web/v7/formmaster_session.html';
---

<Base title="Practice" description="Run a timed cross-contour practice session in your browser.">
  <section class="page wrap">
    <p class="label">Practice session</p>
    <h1 class="title">Begin your practice</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Select your <strong>preferred settings</strong> for the session, including the type of feedback and
      duration.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap game-wrap" aria-label="Loop Master practice session">
    <div class="game">
      <iframe
        src={game}
        title="Loop Master practice session"
        loading="lazy"
        allow="fullscreen"
        sandbox="allow-same-origin allow-popups allow-top-navigation-by-user-activation allow-scripts allow-modals allow-downloads allow-pointer-lock allow-presentation"
      ></iframe>
    </div>
    <p class="game-links label">
      <a href={game} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full window ↗</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <a href="https://zinnia-art.itch.io/loop-master" rel="noopener">Use Loop Master on itch.io ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap guide">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="label">How to practice</p>
      <h2>Instructions for the Practice Session</h2>
      <p>Although nothing is absolute, here are some instructions that might help you in your practice:</p>

      <h3>Before starting</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Choose your session settings.</strong> Select your preferred settings for the session,
          including the type of feedback and duration.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>During the session</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Draw the shape first.</strong> Begin by drawing the shape shown on the screen. Focus on capturing
          its overall form and proportions accurately.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Visualize the cross contour lines.</strong> Once you have drawn the shape, take a moment to
          visualize the cross contour lines. Imagine how these lines would wrap around the form, following its
          curves and contours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Draw the complete or visible cross contour lines.</strong> Decide whether you want to draw the
          complete cross contour lines or just the visible ones. If drawing only the visible lines, make sure they
          “hug” the shape, wrapping around its surface to convey depth and volume.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <figure class="example">
      <Image src={loopRight} alt="A cylinder with one orange cross-contour loop around its middle; the hidden back half is dashed" widths={[400, 800]} sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 400px" />
      <figcaption class="label">A complete loop: visible half solid, hidden half dashed.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap guide tips">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="label">Tips</p>
      <h2>Tips for Drawing Cross Contour Lines</h2>
      <h3>Visualize the full form</h3>
      <p>
        Even if you’re only drawing the visible cross contour lines, mentally visualize the entire form,
        including the hidden lines. This helps in creating a more accurate and convincing depiction.
      </p>
      <h3>Draw complete contour lines</h3>
      <p>
        Practice drawing the full contour lines around the object, not just the visible parts. This will help you
        understand the form better and improve your ability to visualize 3D shapes.
      </p>
      <h3>Hug the shape</h3>
      <p>
        When drawing visible cross contour lines, make sure they “hug” the shape, following its curves and
        contours closely. Avoid drawing straight lines; instead, let the lines wrap around the form to convey
        depth and volume.
      </p>
      <h3>Consistency is key</h3>
      <p>
        Make drawing cross contour lines a regular part of your practice routine. Consistency will help you
        develop a stronger understanding of form and improve your overall drawing skills.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="example">
      <Image src={loopCompare} alt="Two loops on a cylinder: a curved one marked correct, a flat straight one marked wrong" widths={[400, 800]} sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 400px" />
      <figcaption class="label">Curve with the form (✓), not straight across it (✗).</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .title { font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 5rem); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .lede strong { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }

  .game-wrap { padding-block: 24px 0; }
  .game {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    min-height: 480px;
    max-height: 85svh;
    background: #000;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-dim);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .game iframe {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    border: 0;
  }
  .game-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px 12px; margin-top: 14px; }
  .game-links a { color: var(--line); }

  .guide {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 380px);
    gap: clamp(32px, 6vw, 96px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .tips { padding-top: 0; }
  .example { margin: 0; position: sticky; top: 32px; }
  .example img { width: 100%; border-radius: var(--radius); }
  .example figcaption { margin-top: 10px; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .guide { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .example { position: static; max-width: 420px; }
  }
</style>
